nt_states shows only R4.1-R4.3 remaining as ready, meaning the earlier requirements must have already been finished.\n\nSo the key insight is that requirement_states only lists SRDs with work still pending—anything not there has already been completed. To prioritize by release, I need to identify which release has the earliest SRDs with remaining requirements in the ready state.\n\nLooking at the mapping, srd017-tee is the earliest with work remaining, targeting rel02.1. The rest of the pending SRDs are spread across rel02.2 through rel04.1, with most of their requirements already in ready state.\n\nI'm noticing a gap though—cat and sponge are listed in code_status for rel01.1 and rel01.2, but I don't see corresponding SRDs in the requirement_states. I need to figure out if cat has an SRD like srd006-cat or srd007-cat that I'm missing, or if it's tracked differently.\n\nActually, the instruction is clear: I should only propose tasks for R-items with \"ready\" status from the SRDs listed in requirement_states. So I need to focus on those and ignore anything not explicitly tracked there. Looking at what's actually marked as ready, rel02.1 has srd017-tee with R4.1-R4.3 in ready state, but the code_status shows tee as already implemented—so there's a mismatch I need to reconcile. Now I'm working through the priority queue and need to validate against weight constraints. Since the actual requirements.yaml file isn't available in the project context, I'll assume each requirement has a default weight of 1 and check that no task exceeds the 4-weight limit before proceeding with implementation.\n\nFor the first task, I'm considering srd017-tee with requirements R4.1 through R4.3—these are follow-up features for the tee module that already has recent implementation work. However, I realize I don't have the actual SRD content to understand what these specific requirements entail.\n\nLooking at the commit history, I can see the progression: basic functionality was completed fir