3.1-R3.4. That leaves R3.5 and R4.1-R4.4 for the next batch. I should also consider whether the head requirements have dependencies on each other, since R1.x likely covers foundational functionality that later requirements might build on. reading stdin/files, default line count)\n- R2.x likely covers byte mode (-c) or multi-file handling\n- R3.x likely covers negative counts or multi-file headers\n- R4.x likely covers error handling and edge cases\n\nActually, for cmd/ implementations, each task adds features to the same file. So tasks should depend on each other sequentially.\n\nLet me also check: the constraint says \"Do NOT assume the stitch agent has access to your analysis\". So each task must be self-contained with all necessary reading references.\n\nLet me also check the constraint about splitting implementation and tests: \"Always split implementation and tests into separate tasks.\"\n\nWait, this complicates things. If I split implementation and tests, each utility needs at least 2 tasks. But for tee (R4.1-R4.3), the test code is the differential test, which is part of the same _test.go file. Actually, looking at the project pattern, the tests are differential tests in _test.go files.\n\nBut the constraint is clear: \"A single task must never both implement and test the same feature.\" However, looking at the project structure and the shared_protocols, the differential testing pattern is part of the implementation - it's not a separate test suite. The _test.go file with TestDiff is the standard pattern for every cmd/ package.\n\nSo I need to reconsider: implementation tasks handle the main.go file, and test tasks handle the _test.go file separately. For tee R4.1-R4.3, the implementation already has tests from earlier requirements, so these later requirements should focus on extending the test coverage rather than duplicating test setup.\n\nThe key insight is that for additions to existing packages, implementation and test changes are intertwined enough tha