g these 3 remaining requirements into implementation and test is overly granular and would create tiny tasks. But the constraint says I can't implement and test the same feature in one task, so I need to respect that strictly.\n\nLet me think about this differently. Looking at the differential testing pattern, the test file just calls `RunDiffTests` with test cases, so the test file IS the verification. When I implement a feature in main.go, I verify it by adding test cases to _test.go. These are inherently coupled, but the rule is explicit, so I'll follow it. For tee's remaining 3 requirements, I'll split them as: Task 0 handles the R4.1-R4.3 implementation changes to main.go, and Task 1 adds the corresponding test cases to tee_test.go with a dependency on Task 0.\n\nFor head with 17 requirements as a new package, Task 2 creates main.go with R1.1-R1.4 implementation (depending on the tee tasks being done), and Task 3 creates head_test.go with the corresponding tests, depending on Task 2. This uses all 4 tasks but only gets through tee completion and the first few head requirements.\n\nWait, I'm overthinking this. Let me reread the constraint about line counts. A test-only task adding differential test cases might be 50-100 lines, well below the 250-350 target for production code. That's a mismatch I need to resolve.\n\nLooking at the constraints again, there's a slight contradiction between the 250-350 range and the 300-500 range mentioned elsewhere. I'll aim for 300-400 lines as a middle ground. For tee's R4.1-R4.3 requirements, the changes to main.go and tee_test.go combined would only be 100-200 lines, so splitting them into separate tasks would create tasks that fall short of the target size.\n\nI think the intent of the line limit is really for new features and packages rather than small additions to existing code. But I should follow the constraint as written. Let me step back and reconsider the whole approach. With a maximum of 4 tasks, I want to prioritize 